WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.750
- Welcome all to our final
event of the semester

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.800
and of the year.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.770
My name is Dr. Ty Kamp,
and I'm assistant director

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.700
of the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.140
We're delighted that this year

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.900
we've started gathering
in person once again

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.740
for our signature public talks
and convivial receptions,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.990
I think, for which we are known.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.750
It's through the magnanimity
of our benefactors

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.260
Mr. and Mrs. Richard
Franke and Barbara Franke

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.650
that we have produced
such robust programming,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.490
talks ranging from such
diverse topics as overfishing

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.820
on the Mexican Peninsula
to the Salvator Mundi,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.320
the Leonardo da Vinci's lost
painting we learned about

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.460
a few months ago, to the
history of CGI in movies,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.600
to novel, interdisciplinary
approaches to architecture

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.910
and the built environment.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.400
We hope you've enjoyed this year of talks

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.850
and intellectual exchange at
the boundaries ever permeable

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.570
between the sciences and the humanities.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.600
Before I introduce today's
talk by Dr. Michael Caplan,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.140
I'm required to give this
rather officious notice.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.340
Yale University is photographing
and videotaping this event.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.790
There's a possibility you may
be included in these visuals,

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.520
so by being present
during these activities,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.970
you consent to the use of
your appearance, likeness,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.650
and voice by Yale University

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.780
for standard, nonprofit,

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.600
educational, news-related promotional

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.883
and/or editorial purposes.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.780
It's rather legalistic, but
our videographer is a lawyer,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.253
so thank you, Guy.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.103
Could you tell?

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.710
Also, as per usual, we shall be gathering

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.310
for our dinner reception
upstairs in Fellows Hall tonight,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.160
not the student lounge,
but Frederick Young,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.860
Frederick will shepherd
you there after the event,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.230
so please do stay for dinner.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.780
Our guest today is
Professor Michael Caplan,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.970
the C.N.H. Long Professor

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.850
of cellular and molecular physiology,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.510
and professor of cell biology
at Yale School of Medicine,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.310
where he's also the chair

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.310
of cellular and molecular physiology.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:21.480
Michael received his bachelor's
degree from Harvard in 1980,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.480
and his MD and PhD
degrees from Yale in 1987,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.850
so we can praise him on that
proper academic trajectory,

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.710
going from Harvard to Yale.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.280
Sorry, I couldn't help it.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.910
He joined Yale's department of
cellular molecular physiology

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:43.530
as a faculty member in 1988,
and has received fellowships

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.140
from the Helen Hay Whitney Foundation,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.690
the David and Lucile Packard Foundation

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.340
for science and engineering,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.920
and a national Young Investigator Award

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.860
from the National Science Foundation.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.770
He has also received the
Young Investigator Awards

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.530
from the American Physiological Society

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.040
and the American Society of Nephrologists.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.950
Michael's work focuses
on understanding the ways

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.050
in which kidney cells organize

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.020
and maintain their unique structures.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.290
His laboratory also studies
the mechanisms responsible

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.190
for autosomal dominant
polycystic kidney disease,

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.090
and they're working to identify
targets for new therapies,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.150
really groundbreaking and applicable work

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.280
of import for us all.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.360
So his biography, it's all as
cerebral as it is laudatory,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.670
but I can tell you from
personal experience,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.100
having worked in his lab many years ago,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.500
that Michael is every bit as generous

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.640
and supportive a mentor and
professor as he is accomplished

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.500
and absolutely brilliant,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.770
the evidence of which
you will soon discover.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.410
He's also very, very funny,
so I hope he'll share

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.380
some of his brand of humor with us.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.440
As this is our final
gathering until September,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.750
when, by the way, we shall
be hosting a screening

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.400
of "My Octopus Teacher,"

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.010
maybe you've heard of this from Netflix,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.810
we'll be bringing in
the filmmaker for that,

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.483
so put it on your
calendar, September 14th.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.250
I just wanna take a little
bit of time to recognize

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.080
some very important colleagues of ours,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.580
some of whom are here.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.100
Tony Sudol, whose technical expertise

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.323
makes our events possible.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.620
Rick Leone and his
colleagues at Yale Broadcast,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.940
especially our videographer
today, Guy Ortoleva.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.220
They record our events and they turn them

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.520
into wonderful videos that
receive many, many views

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.030
on Yale's YouTube channel and our website.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.390
Matthew Jacob and the staff
at Coromandel Indian Cuisine

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.670
who cater our receptions,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.690
David Suraci and his family
at Fitzgerald's Florist

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.480
in West Haven because they
ensure that our program

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.270
has arguably the best floral arrangements

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.620
of all Yale events, and
that's pretty high standard.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.500
And also my colleagues at
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.890
who lend us tremendous
support each and every event

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.927
and throughout the year,
Matthew Hagan in particular,

00:05:08.927 --> 00:05:13.170
Inessa Laskova, Sandra
Malan-Bowles, Diane Brown,

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:17.310
they do wonderful work and they
do their jobs enviably well.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.910
And finally, Tanisha Leal and
Jamar, the custodial staff,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.160
for their wonderful
support and professionalism

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.630
for all our events.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.200
So without further ado, Michael,
would you please come up

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.904
for your talk, and please
join me in welcoming him.

00:05:31.904 --> 00:05:34.987
(audience clapping)

00:05:36.031 --> 00:05:38.280
- Thank you so much.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.990
So thank you all for coming,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.960
and thanks especially
to Ty for the invitation

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.063
and the opportunity to talk to you today.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.100
I am going to try to convince
you that the old expression,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.430
art imitates life, can be
read in both directions,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.490
and that life imitates art also,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.130
and that some of what's
going on inside of us

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.750
and inside of ourselves

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:05.750
is extraordinarily aesthetically
pleasing and can teach us,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.370
by looking at it as an aesthetic object,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.550
it can teach us something,
not only about how it works,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.860
but also why it's there.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.250
But I wanna begin with a
little bit of an introduction

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.950
to the discipline we're
gonna be talking about

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.750
and its history.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.423
So the question is, what is physiology?

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.870
These are two of my favorite illustrations

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.840
to try and address that question.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.750
The picture on the right,
which we're gonna talk about

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:38.580
a little bit more, is from
the first systematic study

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.920
of anatomy by Vesalius.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.220
It was drawn in the
1600s, and I love the idea

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.013
of a skeleton contemplating itself.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.060
And to the left, there's a picture

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.490
by the wonderful graphic artist Ben Shahn,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.830
which is referred to as "The Scientist."

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.500
And again, for me,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.280
the integration of the
image of the scientist

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.650
with the work that the scientist is doing

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.900
is especially pleasing.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.990
So physiology is arguably the oldest

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.670
of the biomedical sciences.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:15.090
Depending on where you
wanna start counting,

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:20.090
it could arguably be
credited back to Hippocrates,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.500
about 2,400 years ago,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.020
and it could be credibly said

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.073
that Hippocrates was
the first physiologist.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:35.570
Subsequently Galen, about
in the second century CE,

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:38.910
was the first person to
really develop theories

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.670
about the circulation of
the blood in the body,

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:45.450
and he had the opportunity
to develop his theories

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:46.740
by watching what happened

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.460
to some of the less successful
of the Roman gladiators,

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.400
and that allowed him to learn a little bit

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.480
about what happened

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.293
when blood was allowed to
leave its normal vessels.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.880
In the 1500s, again, we have Vesalius,

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.460
whose name actually means the weasel.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.340
That was the family coat
of arms, and actually,

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.180
if you visit

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.570
the Yale School of Medicine's
historical library,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.570
his coat of arms is on the far wall,

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.880
and it's three weasels chasing each other.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:18.990
Don't ask me why.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.850
I don't know if he gave himself that name

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.710
or if he owed some debts

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.963
and it was given to him by his creditors,

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:31.500
but Vesalius wrote the first
systematic book of anatomy

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:35.190
called "De Humani Corporis
Fabrica Libri Septum."

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:39.540
This is a picture of a dissection going on

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.540
in Vesalius' laboratory,
which was in Bologna in Italy.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.590
You can still visit it today,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:49.350
and it's an extraordinary anatomy theater.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.950
Fortunately, it no longer
smells as it probably did

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.203
at the time that this sketch was made.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.640
And you can see Vesalius right here,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.080
who's leading the dissection.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.110
So Vesalius was really the first person

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.450
who tried to understand anatomy,

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:09.600
and relate it to physiology
and how things work.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.657
And this is the frontispiece
from that first anatomy,

00:09:13.657 --> 00:09:15.480
"De Corpore Humanis."

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.450
And there is actually an
original copy of this book,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.310
which is entirely priceless,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.440
in Yale's medical historical library.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.140
So I encourage you to go to the library,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.150
I encourage you to ask to see it,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.832
and I encourage you to not be
disappointed when they say no.

00:09:30.832 --> 00:09:34.110
(audience laughing)

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.750
William Harvey's book on
the circulation of the blood

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:40.620
from the early 1600s,
Harvey was at Oxford,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.280
and he was the first person
who really began to figure out

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.950
the relationship between
the heart, the lungs,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.190
and the pulmonary and the
systemic circulations.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.850
Although it can be said
that Arab scholars,

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.940
including Ibn as-Nafis,
proposed similar ideas

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.643
much earlier in the 13th century.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.080
Luigi Galvani, who also worked in Bologna,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.170
and whose laboratory you can also visit,

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.800
around the time of the
enlightenment in the mid 1700s,

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.260
he and his wife, working together,

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.480
discovered that electrical signals

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.490
prompt muscle contraction.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.470
And it was really he and his work,

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.410
or he and his wife and
their work that gave rise

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.480
to the concept of bioelectricity

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.370
and the field of electrophysiology,

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.200
which is one of the most prominent fields

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.390
in biomedical science today.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.100
And it's interesting to think about

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.390
what was the physiologic system
that Galvani chose to study.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.610
And those of you who are looking carefully

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.640
can see his experimental
preparation down here.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.410
It is half a frog.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:47.280
So Galvani used the
bottom halves of frogs.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.100
And back in the day of Benjamin Franklin,

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:53.700
instead of flying a kite,
he attracted electricity

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.080
and hooked it up to a frog.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.970
You can see two different frogs

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.040
hooked up to lightning rods here,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.430
and he saw that atmospheric
static electricity

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.790
was able to cause twitchy
movements in the frog legs,

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.290
giving rise to the idea

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.380
that electricity was somehow important

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.360
in how our muscles contracted,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.510
and ultimately, in how nerves conducted.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.480
And it wasn't really until the 1950s,

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.720
the work of Hodgkin-Huxley
in cats in England

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.050
that led us to understand
how electricity is coupled

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:27.453
to the action of nerves and muscles.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.260
More recently, William
Beaumont can be seen

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.950
as the first American to
pursue studies of physiology.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:36.990
And in fact, if you visit
Yale Medical School,

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.990
when you're done looking at
the Vesalius family crest,

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.060
if you go up a flight of stairs,

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.310
there's a large conference
room at the Medical School

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.520
called the Beaumont Room, with
a portrait of Beaumont in it.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.590
Beaumont actually, as far as anyone knows,

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:56.910
never set foot at Yale,
but he was highly admired,

00:11:56.910 --> 00:12:00.000
and so there's a whole room
and a whole portrait of him.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.820
Beaumont's claim to fame was
that he was in the right place

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:06.360
at the right time, and a
guy named Alexis St. Martin

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.820
was in the wrong place at the wrong time.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.360
So St. Martin was a soldier
in the Canadian military

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.570
who caught a piece of
shrapnel in the abdomen.

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:18.000
He survived, and when the wound healed,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.070
it created, what's called, a fistula

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.670
that connected his stomach
to the outside world.

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:26.070
So this poor guy was walking around

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:27.840
and was able to actually live a normal,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.950
reasonably healthy life, but
gastric juice was dripping

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:33.690
out of this fistula.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.730
Beaumont had the wisdom to recognize

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.460
that maybe by collecting
it and measuring it,

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:42.030
it could tell us something about
what controls the secretion

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:43.650
of material into the stomach.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.260
And in fact, Beaumont
did the first experiments

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:51.260
on the relationship between
the brain and acid secretion

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.470
in the stomach before Pavlov
did his famous experiments

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:56.403
with his dog.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:01.803
So here's a picture of Beaumont draining,

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.063
I don't know, hopefully you
can, well, let me try this.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:15.450
So there's a picture of
Beaumont draining gastric acid

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:19.170
from an unfortunate, but very
patient Alexis St. Martin.

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.920
And I should say that there's
a large color painting

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.690
of Beaumont in the Beaumont Room,

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:27.900
and a small sketch of Alexis St. Martin,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:30.630
even though, in point of
fact, he and his courage

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.123
probably deserve the
lion's share of the credit.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.870
More recently and more
relevant to this talk

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:38.430
are the two people who are seen

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.130
as the founders of modern physiology.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.160
So Claude Bernard was a French scientist

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.430
who coined the term milieu interieur,

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:50.550
and by that he meant that we as organisms

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:55.550
who live in an air environment
are yet made up of 60% water.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.410
So we carry within us a volume of fluid

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.790
that is critically important

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:05.400
for how every cell in our body works,

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.890
and he recognized that a large fraction

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:12.720
of what our bodies do is work
to persist the composition,

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.630
to maintain the composition of that fluid,

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.030
because maintaining the
composition of that fluid

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:19.500
is critically important

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.810
to all of the other physiologic processes

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.640
that get carried out in the body.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.830
So his idea was that the maintenance

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:29.070
of the milieu interieur,
the interior milieu,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:33.000
is the main function of all
of our physiologic systems.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.050
Walter Cannon, who was a scientist

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:41.343
in the first part of the
20th century at Harvard,

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.420
coined the term homeostasis
to mean the same thing.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.540
In other words, we have a
whole series of processes

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:52.680
in our body that exist to
maintain a steady state,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.680
to maintain the composition
of the interior milieu,

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.320
and to ensure that it remains compatible

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.900
with all the lives of our cells.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.840
So what are the challenges
that a cell faces?

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:07.743
For a single-cell organism,
like a paramecium or an ameba,

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:12.030
the cell biological and
physiological processes

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:13.110
are one and the same.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.900
In other words, anything
that the cell needs to do

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:19.110
to stay alive is its physiology,

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:21.360
and they're focused on the same goal,

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:24.690
that is maintaining an
organized internal environment

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.740
that's distinct from the
inhospitable outside world.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.230
If you're a paramecium swimming in a pond,

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:35.190
your main job is to remain
distinct from the pond, right?

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:39.420
You don't wanna dissolve
and end up distributed

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.160
throughout the pond.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.910
So in essence, the major task
for a single-celled organism,

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:48.480
and here I'm going to
tread between science

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.710
and philosophy and
theology, but the major job

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:56.550
of a single-cell organism can
be summed up as harvesting

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.250
and using the energy that it needs

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.490
to maintain the subcellular
structures that it has

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:05.100
that harvest and expend energy.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:09.420
In other words, a paramecium
exists because it can.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.690
I don't, in any way, want
to discount for those of you

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.420
whom it's important for, the
role of divine intervention

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.820
in this whole process, but the point is,

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.800
something like a paramecium exists

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.200
because a paramecium can exist.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.830
It has the structures that
it needs to harvest energy,

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:29.040
whether it's from
sunlight or other sources,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.730
and use that energy to
maintain its composition,

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:37.730
to fight off the urge to
burst and fuse with the pond.

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:43.280
So the challenge for a
paramecium is how to use energy

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:45.483
to solve that problem.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.810
So on the scale of a single-cell organism,

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.650
diffusion is fast enough to
ensure that small molecules,

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:58.080
the nutrients that this
paramecium is gonna eat,

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:01.740
for example, can be absorbed
across the cell surface

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.500
and readily distributed
throughout the whole cell,

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.570
and we call the makeup

00:17:06.570 --> 00:17:09.330
of the inside of a cell the cytoplasm.

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.850
So the cytoplasm can receive nutrients

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:14.010
directly across the cell's surface,

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.590
and that can feed all of
the aspects of the cell

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:17.793
that it needs to feed.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.500
We have exactly the same problem,

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:26.700
but on a hundred thousand
times or more larger scale.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:30.720
So we have to harvest
the energy that we need

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.640
to maintain all of our systems.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.900
Again, our main problem
is to overcome entropy,

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.950
to overcome the inevitable forces

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:44.163
that would otherwise lead to
our dissolution and decay.

00:17:45.225 --> 00:17:47.580
For those of you who are interested

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:52.170
in graves of famous scientists,
not far from where we are,

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:55.950
in the Grove Street Cemetery, is the grave

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:58.050
of a guy named Jay Willard Gibbs,

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:00.540
who was one of the
founding faculty members

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.900
of the Sheffield School, which
subsequently fused with Yale

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:05.220
and became, essentially,

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:07.710
the colonel of its science departments.

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:11.490
Gibbs was sort of the founder
of modern thermodynamics

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.440
and developed an equation,

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.693
coincidentally named the Gibbs equation,

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:21.620
that explains the relationship
between energy and entropy.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:25.017
And for those who are
truly devoted to this

00:18:25.017 --> 00:18:26.550
and who are interested in doing

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.380
a very interesting grave rubbing,

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:30.390
that equation is on his headstone

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:32.043
in the Grove Street Cemetery.

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.690
At any rate, we need to invest energy

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:38.850
to overcome the forces of entropy

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:42.630
and to maintain our
composition and our structure.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.850
But because we are far too big

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:46.950
for diffusion to be sufficient

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:50.670
to carry all of the things
we need around our bodies,

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:54.210
we have very complicated
circulatory system

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:56.010
and distributed organ functions

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.050
that make all of that possible.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:00.570
So whereas the paramecium can get by

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:03.360
having everything in
one cell, in one place,

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.940
and surviving by diffusion alone,

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:09.450
we have distributed functions
in the form of organs,

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:12.000
each of those organs
connected to each other

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.290
by a circulatory system,
and each of those organs

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:19.860
subserving a specified individual function

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.120
or series of individual functions
that, were we paramecium,

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:26.973
would be subserved by a
subcellular structure.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.100
For both single-cell organisms

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:35.550
and for complex multicellular
organisms like us,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.980
maintaining compositional integrity

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:41.190
involves controlling the
movement and small molecules

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:43.230
into and out of cells.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.650
Obviously, if you're a paramecium
floating around in a pond,

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:49.440
it's important that water doesn't flow in,

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:52.200
swell you up and cause you to explode,

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.690
but it's important also
that you can import

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:57.240
whatever nutrients you want from that pond

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.550
so that you can eat and you can export

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:01.473
whatever waste products you generate.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:06.480
In the case of us, we
are composed of cells,

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.180
trillions of cells,
and each of those cells

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:12.330
confronts the same
problem as a paramecium.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:15.240
Each of those cells, for
all intents and purposes,

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:18.240
lives in a pond or a saltwater bath

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:21.780
that is, what we call, our
extracellular fluid compartment.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.590
So a 60-kilogram person is carrying around

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.170
about 36 liters of water,

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:31.023
and that 36 liters of
water is about 0.9% saline,

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:36.120
so about a 10th the
concentration of sea water,

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:40.200
and maintaining that composition
is critically important

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:44.190
to maintaining cell function,
and each cell has to be able

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:47.670
to exchange with that extracellular fluid

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.540
what it needs to live on and
what it needs to get rid of.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:52.680
In your bloodstream

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:56.010
and in your extracellular
fluid compartment is glucose

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:58.260
that your cell needs to create energy,

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:01.080
or amino acids that the
cell needs to make proteins,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.360
the building blocks of the cell structure,

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:07.530
or nucleic acids that the cell
needs to make the DNA and RNA

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.980
that it uses to store and
communicate information.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:13.860
The cell is taking all of
that up from outside of it,

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:16.980
from the extracellular fluid
compartment, and again,

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:20.310
getting rid of any waste
products it might generate.

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:23.550
So each of our cells, for
all intents and purposes,

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.440
has exactly the same problem

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:28.773
as these paramecia, paramecii,

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:33.480
and they need to figure out
how to exchange components

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.580
with an outside environment,

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:39.840
but maintain their own individual
compositional integrity.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:43.200
So really, the focus of
this talk is gonna be

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:46.080
what are the cells that
form the interfaces

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:49.140
between our bodies and the outside world?

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:51.480
What problems do they need to solve?

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:54.780
And what structures allow
them to solve these problems?

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:58.740
So I'm gonna introduce you
now to my favorite cell type,

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:00.930
and these are epithelial cells.

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:04.350
Epithelial cells are the
cells that form the barrier

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:07.110
between the inside of you
and the outside of you.

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:08.700
And of course, when I say the barrier

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:10.620
between the inside of you
and the outside of you,

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:13.230
the first thing you think of is your skin,

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:14.280
and that's a good guess.

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.410
The skin is an epithelium,

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.230
but we have many more epithelia as well.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:23.220
So if you think about it,
the lumen of your gut,

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.220
the inside of your intestine
is really continuous

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:27.930
with the outside world.

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:30.570
If you were flexible
enough and did enough yoga,

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:33.780
you could turn yourself
inside out around your gut

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.090
and still be topologically equivalent.

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:38.820
In other words, the inside
of your gut is continuous

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:42.510
with the outside world, and
the cells that line your gut

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:44.697
have to form a barrier
between the inside of you

00:22:44.697 --> 00:22:46.440
and the outside of you.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.360
Similarly, the cells in your airway,

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.410
your trachea, your lungs
confront the outside world.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:54.990
Somewhat more subtly,
the cells in your kidney

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:57.210
do exactly the same thing.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:23:01.190
So there are all different
variety of epithelial cells

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:03.960
in your body that are forming
all of these different

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:05.730
and interesting interfaces,

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.910
and their structure beautifully
reflects their function,

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.220
both in forming the interface

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:15.510
and not being simply a
stupid impermeable barrier,

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.540
but being an interface
that is able to communicate

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:21.420
with its environment,
import what it needs,

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:24.420
and export what it needs to get rid of.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.420
So epithelial cells are the
barriers between the internal

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:29.310
and external compartments.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.530
They control the composition and body,

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:33.933
or the volume of body fluid compartments,

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.440
and they are critically important

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:40.320
to how we maintain our homeostasis.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:42.720
I told you that you are composed

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:45.093
of about 36 liters of fluid.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:49.920
Your kidneys are filtering
all of that fluid

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:51.690
five times a day.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.340
So whether you know it or not,

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:57.180
you are making 180 liters
of filtrate in your kidney.

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.400
Now, I don't know any of you very well,

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:02.580
except for my wife and
parents who are sitting here,

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:05.010
but I'm gonna guess that you produce

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.650
about two liters of urine a day.

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:11.670
That means that your
kidney has to reabsorb 99%

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:13.260
of what it filters.

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.720
It's the epithelial cells that do that.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:18.540
It's the epithelial cells
that are transporting

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.910
all of that salt and water,

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:24.390
and their structure is
critically connected

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:26.703
to how they're able to
do that, as we'll see.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:29.850
So what do epithelial cells look like?

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.020
What do they do?

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.570
And what are the
morphological or structural

00:24:33.570 --> 00:24:35.220
and functional specializations

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.113
that let them perform their roles?

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.950
So let's start with what do
epithelial cells look like.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:44.250
They come in lots of
different shapes and sizes.

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:48.630
In cell biology, we define
epithelial cells as squamous,

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.690
that means flat, pancake-like,

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:54.870
and then there are cuboidal
and columnar epithelial cells,

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:56.820
and then, what we call, pseudo stratified,

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:58.920
and I'll show you examples of all of them.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:04.110
What we're looking at here is
frog skin viewed from the top,

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:05.730
and hopefully you share with me,

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:07.470
oh, sorry, you don't see that.

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:10.710
Hopefully you share
with me an appreciation

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.830
for just how exquisite this pattern is.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.440
You see these pigment
granules in some of the cells,

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:20.220
and you see that these cells
form a tessellation, a tiling,

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:24.000
a hexagonal array of
cells that fit together

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:26.100
and form a beautiful boundary

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:29.130
that separates the frog from its pond.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.730
And these images actually inspired me

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:36.940
to hang a piece of artwork on my wall

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:40.740
that's by Paul Klee,
called "Ad Parnassus,"

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.800
and this is the same image
that was used in the the flyer

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:47.100
that Ty beautifully
prepared for this talk.

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:49.770
This is an image by
Paul Klee that, for me,

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:53.340
reminds me very much of the
structure of an epithelial cell.

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:57.000
Individual cells linked
together in a tiling,

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.010
in a tessellation, and each one

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.330
with its own individual color structure

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.200
contributing to a whole.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.420
And again, connecting art to life,

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:10.080
Parnassus in Greek myth is the
mountain where the muses live

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:12.180
and the origin of art and poetry.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:15.480
So for me, I love the
idea that that Parnassus,

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:18.420
at least in this image,
connects up to an epithelium,

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:20.343
which is my favorite kind of tissue.

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.870
So I told you that the
skin is an epithelium.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.480
This is a section of human skin.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:28.650
This is the outside.

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:30.930
This is called the stratum corneum,

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:33.600
and this is what faces the outside world.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:38.600
This is the stuff that is forming

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:41.610
the thickest part of the barrier

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:44.880
that prevents the outside world
from penetrating into you.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:48.210
It's also the part that
flakes off from time to time

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:51.120
because this stratum corneum is formed

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:54.120
when the cells lower
down in the epidermis,

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:57.150
mature, flatten out, and die.

00:26:57.150 --> 00:26:58.590
So here's the epidermis.

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:00.690
These are the cells
that are gonna give rise

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:04.560
to the outer layer of the
skin, and these cells undergo

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:07.350
an amazing process of differentiation.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:11.880
In other words, of acquiring
a specialized appearance

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:13.710
to fulfill their function.

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:16.380
They start out, and
hopefully you can see this,

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.860
as cuboidal cells, and as they migrate up

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:22.260
towards the surface, they flatten out

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:25.470
into these pancake-like squamous cells,

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.460
and then become this dead layer

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:33.030
of impermeable material that acts

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:34.740
more or less like a wetsuit

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:37.170
to keep the outside world away from us

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:39.783
and the inside world
away from the outside.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:43.740
There's beautiful work on the
differentiation of skin cells

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:46.560
being done here at Yale by Valentina Greco

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:48.363
at the medical school, for example.

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:52.050
So we talked a little bit about

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:53.880
what epithelial cells can look like.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.330
I showed you skin, and I also
told you that they can adopt

00:27:57.330 --> 00:27:59.340
many different shapes and sizes

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:03.270
that reflect their adaptation
to physiologic roles.

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:06.870
So these are epithelial
cells from the gallbladder.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:08.130
What I hope you can see is that,

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:13.130
opposed to those skin cells,
these guys are really tall,

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:16.080
and they also form this hexagonal array.

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:17.250
What you're looking at here is,

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:20.010
what's called, scanning
electron microscopy.

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.920
It's a specialized form
of electron microscopy

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.470
that allows us to
reconstruct cellular shapes

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.270
in three dimensions.

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:30.990
What I love about this picture,
what I find really striking,

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:33.810
is if any of you who've
had the good fortune

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:36.300
to travel to Scotland or Ireland

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:39.900
and seen the basaltic
columns, the volcanic columns

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:43.290
of the Giant's Causeway or Fingal's Cave,

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:47.880
you can see the resemblance
between these microscopic cells

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.460
and these macroscopic enormous structures

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.533
that are formed as lava cools.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.810
And the shapes develop for
essentially the same reason,

00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:00.660
which is that hexagons are a great way

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:04.080
to minimize the energy of things
that need to fit together.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:05.940
Coming back again to Gibbs,

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:07.833
who's buried a few blocks from here,

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:14.000
I find this as a beautiful
example of natural objects

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:19.203
assuming similar structures
at hugely different scales,

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:24.570
and again, seeing the the
connections in these shapes

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:27.510
and the shapes that
we've adopted imitating

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:29.340
these very natural forms

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:31.803
to carry out these physiologic functions.

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:35.640
These are cells of the intestine.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:38.610
They absorb nutrients, and we'll talk more

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:41.040
about these kinds of
cells in a little bit.

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:45.060
You can see that these are
very tall columnar cells,

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:47.250
and they have this pink
little fuzz on them,

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:48.720
and I'm gonna show you in a minute

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:50.340
what that fuzz is made of.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:54.330
It's actually hundreds of
individual hairs on each cell,

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:57.570
called microvilli, that
increase the surface area

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.320
of these cells and increase
their ability to absorb things,

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:04.380
'cause your intestine is
there to absorb what you eat,

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:07.860
and the more surface area you
have, the more you can absorb,

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:11.343
and these microvilli help
increase that surface area.

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:15.180
These are cells of the airway.

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:18.090
You can see that they have
a different kind of hair

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:18.960
on their surface.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:21.240
These hairs are called cilia.

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:24.150
You notice that some of
the cells look different.

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:27.450
There are these cells called
goblet cells or mucus cells

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:30.480
that have this clear goop
in the middle of them,

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.400
and they secrete mucus.

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:34.440
They're secreting mucus into your airway,

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:37.530
and you may wonder,
especially at allergy season,

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.450
why do we have mucus in our airway

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:42.300
that seems aesthetically unpleasing,

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:43.440
and the reason is that

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:46.200
because we are constantly
breathing in stuff

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:51.000
that we don't want in our
body, dust, bacteria, viruses,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.370
if you have a nose like mine, small birds,

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:58.370
and we need to keep
that out of our airways.

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:03.270
The mucus that these cells
secrete lines our airways

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:05.793
and catches that stuff like fly paper.

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:09.570
What I'm gonna show you in a
little bit is that the cilia

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:14.570
on top of these cells are
actually beating like little oars,

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.640
and by beating, they're moving that mucus

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:19.200
up towards our mouth and nose

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:21.840
so that we can cough or sneeze it out.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.600
It's called the ciliary escalator.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:28.680
So these motile cilia
are critically important

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:32.880
to helping us keep pathogens
from entering our bodies

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:34.530
and helping us to get rid of them.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:38.610
Again, specialized adaptations
that allow us to live

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:40.260
in a hostile world.

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:43.320
So these cells are called
columnar epithelia.

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.810
These are pseudo stratified,

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:50.640
pseudo stratified because
these blue blobs, the nuclei,

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:54.123
don't line up in these
cells the way they do here.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:57.270
It looks like you have
multiple layers of cells,

00:31:57.270 --> 00:31:59.730
but in fact, it's one layer

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:03.573
that just is not as well
vertically organized.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:08.550
So what do epithelial cells
do and how do they do it?

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.643
So I need to introduce you
now to some cell biology.

00:32:13.470 --> 00:32:16.620
This is a diagram of a
more or less typical cell,

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:19.560
and the first thing I wanna
explain to you is that

00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:23.160
just as our body is
surrounded by a layer of skin,

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:24.990
cells have a skin,

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:27.840
but that skin is something
called a plasma membrane

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:32.010
or a cell membrane, and that
membrane forms the boundary

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:34.830
between the inside of the cell
and the outside of the cell.

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:37.173
So it's shown here in pink.

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:42.840
And this pink membrane
is both going to maintain

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:45.210
the compositional integrity of the cell

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:47.550
and determine what gets into it

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:49.830
and what gets expelled from it.

00:32:49.830 --> 00:32:52.020
You can also see that there
are lots of other goodies

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:55.650
inside of this cell, lots of
other subcellular structures

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.963
that we're gonna talk
about in greater detail.

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:02.190
There's a nucleus, and most
of you are probably familiar

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:04.590
with the idea that the nucleus of the cell

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:06.690
is where the DNA is stored,

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:10.530
and DNA carries the instruction
manual that the cell uses

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:14.970
both to reproduce itself, and
also to encode the production

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:17.970
of every one of the cell's components.

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:20.800
The nucleus is where
the instruction manual

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:25.593
for the cell is stored and
where it's opened and read.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:29.850
The cell also contains mitochondria.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:32.730
Mitochondria are
energy-generating structures.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:35.910
This is where the oxygen
that we breathe is used

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.990
to help burn glucose to make energy.

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:42.000
So essentially, our body derives

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.730
all of its energy from combustion.

00:33:44.730 --> 00:33:48.870
It's a controlled enzymatic
combustion of glucose,

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:51.060
turning that into carbon dioxide,

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:53.700
and using oxygen to help us do that,

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:56.370
and that happens in this
structure called the mitochondria,

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.653
which we're gonna talk about much more.

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:02.640
I wanna go a little bit deeper

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:06.450
into the structure of a
membrane before we go on.

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:08.550
These membranes have to serve

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:10.140
a couple of different purposes.

00:34:10.140 --> 00:34:12.990
Again, they need to separate
the inside of the cell

00:34:12.990 --> 00:34:15.480
from the outside of the
cell, so it makes sense

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:18.660
that you'd want them to
be impermeable to water.

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:21.273
You don't want water to
be able to leak through,

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:24.570
but on the other hand,
they have to be able

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:28.110
to let important things in
and important things out.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.840
We need to let glucose
into the cell, for example.

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:34.740
We need to expel the acid
byproducts of metabolism

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:36.240
out of the cell.

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:40.440
So the cell membrane has to
be both water impermeable,

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.870
but selectively permeable to things

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:45.390
that dissolve well in water.

00:34:45.390 --> 00:34:47.130
That seems sort of paradoxical.

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:48.660
How do you do that?

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:50.730
So the cell solves this problem

00:34:50.730 --> 00:34:54.480
with something called a
phospholipid bilayer membrane.

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:56.523
Lipids are a kind of fat,

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:00.690
and lipids are, of
course, water insoluble.

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:02.280
All of us have had the experience

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:03.930
of working with salad dressing,

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:06.630
and you know that the
olive oil is floating

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:09.060
on top of the balsamic vinegar

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:11.940
and not interacting closely with it.

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:15.180
Membrane is made of very similar stuff.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:20.180
We know that it's made up of
two layers of phospholipid

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:23.400
because of an experiment that
was done about 100 years ago

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:26.343
by two people in Holland
named Gorter and Grendel.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:29.910
What they did was
extract all of the lipids

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:33.270
from red blood cells,
and they spread that out

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:36.600
on the surface of a water trough,

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.550
and then they measured the surface area

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:41.820
of how much lipid was there,
and they compared that

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.040
to the surface area of the red blood cells

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:46.740
that they had extracted
it from, and they realized

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:49.620
that there was exactly
twice as much surface area

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:52.650
as there was surface area
on the red blood cell,

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:55.320
and that meant it had to be a bilayer.

00:35:55.320 --> 00:35:58.290
Now, in point of fact, they
miscounted their red cells

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:00.660
and they miscalculated the surface area,

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:02.370
but they were compensating errors,

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:05.433
so they came up with the right
answer for the wrong reasons.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:10.170
I will say that this structure
is critically important

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:13.200
to how I ended up making a career choice

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:16.800
because when I was in college,
I took a cell biology class,

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:18.360
and the professor reproduced

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:20.130
the Gorter and Grendel experiment

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.830
with a little bit of an exciting twist.

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:27.830
He spread out, he took a
trough full of methanol,

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:29.433
and he lit it on fire,

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.190
and then he stuck his finger in his ear

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:35.640
to take out some earwax and
dipped it into the trough.

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:38.670
And the idea is that the
earwax is gonna spread out

00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:41.430
on the surface of the
liquid, forming a layer

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:44.190
in the same way that Gorter
and Grendel's lipids did

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:47.310
in their trough, and it
would put out the fire

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:50.490
because that lipid would
be impermeable to oxygen,

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:53.460
and that would've been
dramatic and exciting enough

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.463
if the professor had not
caught his sleeve on fire.

00:36:57.540 --> 00:37:01.350
The vision of a flaming professor
in a cell biology course

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:04.590
was all I needed to dedicate my career.

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:05.553
He survived.

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:09.720
I told you that these
membranes have to function

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:13.140
not only to prevent water
from moving across them,

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:17.160
but also to selectively
let substances like glucose

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:19.740
or ions, et cetera, move through them,

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:21.180
and they do that

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:25.110
because they have embedded
within them proteins.

00:37:25.110 --> 00:37:28.350
Proteins are polymers, they're
made up of amino acids,

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:31.020
and essentially the most
important complicated

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:33.750
and interesting structures in our bodies

00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:35.370
are all made up of proteins.

00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:38.430
Proteins are the engines that
do everything in our body.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:41.400
All of the enzymes you
hear about are proteins.

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:45.000
All of the structures in
cells are composed of proteins

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:47.280
that endow them with both their structure

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:49.830
and functional capacities.

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:54.090
Proteins are embedded in
membranes, and they can serve

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:57.930
as selective tubes or channels
that let stuff diffuse

00:37:57.930 --> 00:37:59.400
into and out of cells.

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.070
In fact, they can be
even smarter than that.

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:05.160
They can use energy to actively pump stuff

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.110
into and out of cells.

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:10.350
So cells can control
their internal environment

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:13.020
by pumping out stuff they wanna get rid of

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.660
or pumping in stuff they need,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:17.850
and they do that with these proteins

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:19.550
that are embedded in the membrane.

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:23.070
These proteins are linked to each other,

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:27.060
and structural integrity
is provided to the membrane

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.810
by virtue of a skeleton,
what's called a cytoskeleton,

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:34.080
that is present beneath
the surface of the membrane

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:35.700
and that acts as a meshwork

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.550
that attaches each of these
proteins to each other.

00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:41.400
Some of the seminal work
on this was done years ago

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:44.790
by Vince Marchesi in the
pathology department here at Yale,

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:48.150
who characterized the
proteins and the interactions

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.610
that mediate the formation of the skeleton

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:51.813
in the red blood cell.

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:56.040
So if we put these components together,

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:57.840
what does a cell look like?

00:38:57.840 --> 00:38:59.010
This is a liver cell.

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:00.540
You can see it has a nucleus,

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:03.480
it has some of the mitochondria
we were talking about,

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:04.920
and it's got other structures

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:08.280
like endoplasmic reticulum,
golgi, lysosomes.

00:39:08.280 --> 00:39:09.780
So let's look at some of these

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:12.450
in a little bit greater detail.

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:13.830
We're now looking in what we call

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:16.140
a transmission electron microscope.

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:19.930
So we've cut a very, very
thin section of a cell

00:39:20.790 --> 00:39:24.570
and we've magnified it
many thousands of times,

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:27.840
and what you're seeing here
is the nucleus of the cell.

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:31.680
The black stuff is the DNA,
the white stuff is material

00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:33.330
in between the DNA,

00:39:33.330 --> 00:39:36.960
and this nucleus forms a defined structure

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:39.723
sitting in the center of the cytoplasm.

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:42.330
You can also see

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:47.330
that there are these smooth
surface membranes out here,

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.600
just next to the nucleus,
and those are what are called

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:54.600
the endoplasmic reticulum,
one of my favorite terms

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:55.560
in cell biology

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:57.930
'cause whenever you say
endoplasmic reticulum,

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:02.163
you sound smart, so it's just
a good word to have on hand.

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:05.790
See these little dots that are sitting

00:40:05.790 --> 00:40:09.720
on the endoplasmic reticulum,
these little black blobs?

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:12.930
These were discovered in
the 1950s by George Palade,

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:14.580
who I was fortunate enough to have

00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:16.890
as one of my thesis advisors.

00:40:16.890 --> 00:40:19.410
And they were identified as the site

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.320
in which the cell makes proteins.

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:26.190
So DNA in the nucleus
carries the instructions

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:29.700
for how we make everything in the cell.

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:31.170
Basically, it's the instructions

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.170
for how every protein in the cell is made.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:38.550
Those instructions get copied
down into a little notebook

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:43.320
that gets exported from the
nucleus, called messenger RNA,

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:48.320
or ribonucleic acid, and ribosomes
sit on that messenger RNA

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:53.580
and read that code, and use
that code to make proteins.

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:56.670
That's what's called the
central dogma of biology.

00:40:56.670 --> 00:41:00.960
The information in the DNA
is transcribed into RNA,

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:04.680
is read by ribosomes to make proteins.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:07.920
Now you see that some of
these ribosomes are sitting

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:10.650
on top of the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:13.870
and they're there to solve
a complicated problem

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:18.990
that I didn't mention earlier
on, but that is associated

00:41:18.990 --> 00:41:21.480
with these proteins being in a membrane.

00:41:21.480 --> 00:41:24.660
How do you get a protein
stuck into a membrane

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:27.120
and organized in the right way?

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:30.840
And the answer is that these
ribosomes can essentially act

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:32.820
as a sewing machine.

00:41:32.820 --> 00:41:37.230
As the protein is being made
and emerges from the ribosome,

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:41.550
it's sewn across the membrane
through a special tube

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:43.830
or tunnel that's present in the membrane

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:47.850
of the endoplasmic reticulum
so that proteins can be woven

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:50.250
into the surface of the membrane

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:53.220
and endow the cells
membranes with the proteins

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.613
that they're gonna need to carry
out all of their functions.

00:41:58.650 --> 00:42:03.650
And actually, it was for
figuring this out that Palade,

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:08.280
along with Günter Blobel,
separately won the Nobel Prize

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:09.873
in physiology and medicine.

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:13.920
Here's endoplasmic reticulum.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:16.710
Here's another structure that
was discovered by Palade,

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:21.210
again, in the 1960s,
called the golgi complex.

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:24.240
Proteins that get made in
the endoplasmic reticulum

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:26.820
get carried in little cargo vesicles,

00:42:26.820 --> 00:42:30.360
little, essentially, packages

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:32.910
that butt off the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:36.465
travel through the cytoplasm,
fuse with the golgi,

00:42:36.465 --> 00:42:37.410
and in the golgi,

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:40.470
those proteins undergo
maturation reactions

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:43.530
to help them be prepared
for their next role.

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:46.980
So the golgi complex is the
next step in the assembly line

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:50.433
as these proteins get made
and travel through the cell.

00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:53.820
These are pancreatic acinar cells.

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:55.260
These are the cells that secrete

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:58.710
all of the digestive enzymes
that your pancreas makes,

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:01.470
and your pancreas stores those enzymes

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:04.173
in these blobs called secretory vesicles.

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:09.420
Jim Jamieson, who was then a
student in George Palade's lab,

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:13.450
wondered if he could figure
out how proteins travel

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:19.830
from the endoplasmic reticulum, uh-oh,

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:23.140
how proteins travel from
the endoplasmic reticulum

00:43:24.958 --> 00:43:26.820
to the golgi complex, to the cell surface.

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:30.840
Because at the time he was
doing those experiments in 1967,

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.070
it was not known.

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:34.670
It was known there was
an endoplasmic reticulum,

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:37.893
a golgi, secretory vesicles,
but how did things travel?

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:42.720
So Jamieson did a really smart experiment.

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:45.510
He took pancreatic acinar

00:43:45.510 --> 00:43:48.150
and fed them radioactive amino acids.

00:43:48.150 --> 00:43:51.603
Now, any protein that's
gonna be made is radioactive,

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:56.220
and at various times after
feeding them radioactivity,

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:59.910
he cut sections of them

00:43:59.910 --> 00:44:03.120
and coated them with a
photographic emulsion.

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:07.020
The photographic emulsion
would deposit silver grains

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:09.750
wherever there were radioactive particles.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:13.500
So basically, by looking at
where the silver grains go,

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:14.460
he could figure out

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:17.880
where the newly synthesized
proteins were traveling.

00:44:17.880 --> 00:44:22.530
So hopefully you can see
these black blobs here

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:25.383
are silver grains over
the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:29.640
And when he waited about 10 minutes or so,

00:44:29.640 --> 00:44:31.860
you could see that now the black blobs

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:35.940
are over the golgi complex,
and if he waited longer,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:40.200
they were in immature and then
mature secretory granules.

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:42.420
Jamieson did that experiment as a student

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:44.160
in George Palade's lab.

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:46.560
He actually did it while
Palade was on vacation

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:49.500
'cause Palade really didn't
want him to do this experiment.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:52.860
He'd set him on another project
that was totally impossible.

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:54.510
Jim wanted to do this experiment.

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:56.070
Palade said no.

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:59.250
Jim did it while George
was away on vacation,

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:01.680
and this result was a big part

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:03.663
of why George won the Nobel Prize.

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:06.390
But Jim received a lot of credit,

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:08.043
so it all worked out in the end.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:12.420
Okay, so I've introduced
you to components of cells.

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:14.730
Now let's see how they come together

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:16.983
in an epithelium like the kidney.

00:45:17.910 --> 00:45:21.750
So this structure here is a glomerulus.

00:45:21.750 --> 00:45:23.730
I told you that your kidney filters

00:45:23.730 --> 00:45:26.340
180 liters of fluid a day.

00:45:26.340 --> 00:45:29.550
You have about a million of
these glomerular structures

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:31.770
in each of your kidneys.

00:45:31.770 --> 00:45:34.860
The filtrate that's formed
from this glomerulus

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:37.290
enters a tubule called the nephron,

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:41.310
or the tubule of the
kidney, and these cells

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:44.070
that line the earliest
part of the nephron are,

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:46.323
what we call, proximal tubule cells.

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:50.490
The fluid flows through
the nephron, and again,

00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:54.180
about 178 liters of it
is gonna get absorbed

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:56.940
by an assembly line series of processes

00:45:56.940 --> 00:46:00.810
that require different
steps at each stage.

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:04.110
And you can see that these
cells of the proximal tubule

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:07.890
look different than cells
of the distal tubule,

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:10.650
look different than cells
of the collecting tubule.

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:13.410
Each of the cell types that
are gonna carry out a function

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.640
are specially adapted,

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:17.310
structurally and functionally, to do that.

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:20.310
I want you to pay
attention to the striations

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:21.860
in the base of this cell.

00:46:21.860 --> 00:46:25.110
Do you see how interestingly striated

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:26.760
the bottom of this cell is?

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:29.043
We're gonna talk about
that more in a second.

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:32.730
This is what these cells look like

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:35.460
in a transmission electron microscope.

00:46:35.460 --> 00:46:38.670
These are the proximal tubule
cells I told you about.

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:40.350
You can see that they've got

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:42.210
something really interesting going on

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:43.653
in the lumen of the tubule.

00:46:44.520 --> 00:46:49.350
You can also see that these
cells are full of mitochondria,

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.060
huge numbers of mitochondria.

00:46:51.060 --> 00:46:55.320
Remember, mitochondria are
where the cells generate energy.

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:57.900
Why does the kidney need so much energy?

00:46:57.900 --> 00:46:59.310
Here in the proximal tubule,

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:03.300
here in the distal tubule,
huge numbers of mitochondria.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:07.950
The answer is that we are
pumping 180 liters of salt water

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:11.880
from the lumen of that kidney
tubule back into your body.

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:13.380
The pumps that do that

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:15.750
burn a huge amount of metabolic energy.

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:20.750
8% of the oxygen consumption
that your body carries out

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:24.120
is used by these
mitochondria and kidney cells

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:28.113
to transport all of that salt
water back into the body.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:33.240
Let's look more closely
at these microvilli,

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:35.970
these structures on the
proximal tubule cells

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.883
that are filling the lumen
of this proximal tubule here.

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:40.920
This is what they look like

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:43.470
in a transmission electron microscope.

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:45.600
You can see they're about a micron long,

00:47:45.600 --> 00:47:47.790
a micron being a millionth of a meter

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:49.950
or a thousandth of a millimeter,

00:47:49.950 --> 00:47:53.430
and there are hundreds of them
on the surface of the cell.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:56.160
So this is them at low magnification.

00:47:56.160 --> 00:47:58.530
This is them at high magnification.

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:00.840
Here in scanning electron microscopy,

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:03.810
you can see this beautiful
brush-like appearance,

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:06.960
and in fact, this is
called the brush border.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:09.960
And if you cut cross sections of them,

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:13.320
this is what each of these
guys looks like in the middle.

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:15.900
There are scaffoldings that
run up the middle of it,

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:17.910
and one of the people who's done the most

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:20.330
to figure out what that
scaffold is made of

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:23.520
is a brilliant scientist here
at Yale named Mark Mooseker,

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:26.100
who's in the department
of molecular, cellular,

00:48:26.100 --> 00:48:28.140
and developmental biology.

00:48:28.140 --> 00:48:30.750
And understanding that
skeleton has been key

00:48:30.750 --> 00:48:33.660
to understanding, providing
us an understanding

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:35.880
of how these cells work.

00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:38.520
I'm showing you here two different modes

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:40.770
of electron microscopy.

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:43.410
Very excitingly, there's now a third mode

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:44.793
of electron microscopy.

00:48:44.793 --> 00:48:46.680
Well, there are many modes
of electron microscopy,

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:49.500
but there's a new one
that's just come to Yale

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:53.400
called focused ion beam
scanning electron microscopy.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:56.460
What we can do now is take
three dimensional images

00:48:56.460 --> 00:48:57.780
through a cell.

00:48:57.780 --> 00:49:01.800
Most of you know or have
experienced what a CAT scan is.

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:05.970
A CAT scan, computerized
tomography, allows an X-ray machine

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:07.590
to generate three dimensional

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:10.260
cross-sectional images through you.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:13.890
Focused ion beam scanning
electron microscopy allows us

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:16.530
to do the same thing on a piece of tissue

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:19.140
on the order of the size of a human hair.

00:49:19.140 --> 00:49:21.930
And so what you're looking
at here is a portion

00:49:21.930 --> 00:49:25.290
of the inside of one of
these proximal tubule cells,

00:49:25.290 --> 00:49:28.440
the orange guys of the
mitochondria, the green guys,

00:49:28.440 --> 00:49:32.280
the endoplasmic reticulum, the
yellow is the cell surface.

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:33.600
And now for the first time,

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:35.820
we can appreciate in three dimensions

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:39.180
how all of these structures
are interacting with each other

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:40.860
and talking to each other.

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.200
And what you can see is there
are tremendous connections

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:44.490
between these structures,

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:47.100
and we're just starting to understand

00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:50.493
how those connections form
and what those connections do.

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:54.780
Now, if epithelial cells
are gonna form a boundary

00:49:54.780 --> 00:49:57.270
between the inside of you
and the outside of you,

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:59.940
they have to be connected to each other.

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:02.640
If you put tiles down on a floor,

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:04.980
those tiles need to be
connected to each other

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:07.230
by some sort of grout or something

00:50:07.230 --> 00:50:09.420
to keep the outside surface

00:50:09.420 --> 00:50:11.880
from getting underneath the tiles.

00:50:11.880 --> 00:50:14.910
Cells have junctions
that hold them together.

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:17.490
They have things called tight junctions

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:20.400
that prevent the inside
world from leaking out

00:50:20.400 --> 00:50:22.593
and the outside world from leaking in,

00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:27.000
and they also have
things called desmosomes

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:29.700
that act as rivets or spot welds,

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:31.140
that hold the cells together

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:33.150
and form the structural integrity

00:50:33.150 --> 00:50:36.360
that allows the epithelium to persist.

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:40.440
So this is a cell biologist's
view of epithelial cells.

00:50:40.440 --> 00:50:43.890
This is a physiologist's
view of epithelial cells.

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:47.700
These are epithelial cells
that are transporting salt.

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:50.280
These are epithelial cells of your kidney,

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:53.520
and they're absorbing
sodium chloride, salt water,

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:56.433
from outside of the
body to inside the body.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:51:00.720
And if you rearrange some
of these same transporters,

00:51:00.720 --> 00:51:03.570
now you have the cells in
the airway or in the gut

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:08.570
that secrete fluid to keep
the mucus in the airway fluid.

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:10.920
So I'm not gonna go into
any of these details,

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:13.590
I just wanna point out
that those same proteins

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:16.320
that I told you about that
are embedded in the membrane

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:21.060
are serving roles as pumps,
as channels, as carriers

00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:23.280
that are moving things
across the membrane,

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:25.170
and where they are in the cell

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:27.273
determines the properties of the cell.

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:31.350
What do those junctions I
told you about look like

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:33.420
in the electron microscope?

00:51:33.420 --> 00:51:34.770
This is a modality called

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:39.450
freeze fracture electron
microscopy, where cells are frozen

00:51:39.450 --> 00:51:42.450
and they're actually hit
with a guillotine blade

00:51:42.450 --> 00:51:45.870
that splits that bilayer
membrane into two.

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:47.970
They're then shadowed with platinum,

00:51:47.970 --> 00:51:51.390
and we can see the platinum
in the electron microscope,

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:55.770
and you can see, hopefully,
this beautiful lattice work.

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:57.990
These lattice work are the tight junctions

00:51:57.990 --> 00:52:00.930
which are acting essentially
as a garden fence

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:04.320
that prevents the inside from
diffusing into the outside,

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:07.230
the outside diffusing into the inside.

00:52:07.230 --> 00:52:10.290
So this structure and the number of lines

00:52:10.290 --> 00:52:13.290
in this lattice work correlate beautifully

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:15.870
with how tight these epithelia are.

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:19.410
The more lines we have, the
higher the resistance to flow

00:52:19.410 --> 00:52:20.853
between these two domains.

00:52:22.080 --> 00:52:24.660
Here's one of those
desmosomes, the spot welds

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:27.510
that give structural
strength to the epithelium,

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:31.050
and you can see that it
looks just like a rivet.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:33.840
So even though it's at
a tiny microscopic scale

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:38.550
on the order of a fraction of a micron,

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:40.530
it looks just like the kinds of rivets

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:42.690
that you might have in your jeans

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:44.223
that hold your pants together.

00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:49.350
I told you about the cilia
that are on the surface

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:53.280
of airway epithelial cells,
these motors that beat

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:57.120
and that move the mucus
towards your mouth and nose.

00:52:57.120 --> 00:53:00.720
The same cilia move
eggs through the oviduct

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:02.703
in advance of fertilization.

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:07.050
So there are critical motile
functions to these cilia,

00:53:07.050 --> 00:53:09.030
and this is what these cilia look like

00:53:09.030 --> 00:53:11.910
in the airway epithelium
in a light microscope,

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:14.940
here's what they look
like in cross-section.

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:17.980
It's an exquisite
structure where you can see

00:53:21.516 --> 00:53:22.630
nine rows of, what are
called, microtubules

00:53:25.530 --> 00:53:28.500
that are connected to each
other by this lattice work

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:31.500
and to a central pair of microtubules.

00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:35.490
And there are motors that
move those microtubules

00:53:35.490 --> 00:53:38.130
that slide against one another

00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:41.190
and that cause these cilia to beat.

00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:43.710
A lot of this was figured
out actually here at Yale

00:53:43.710 --> 00:53:47.370
by Joel Rosenbaum, who
did ground-breaking work

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:51.480
on chlamydomonas, which
is actually a motile algae

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:54.540
and has a tail that allows it to swim.

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:57.690
What Rosenbaum figured
out using chlamydomonas

00:53:57.690 --> 00:54:01.200
turns out to be critically
important to human disease

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:03.780
because there are people
walking around with diseases

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:07.530
called ciliary dyskinesia
or Kartagener's syndrome

00:54:07.530 --> 00:54:09.600
where the cilia don't move,

00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:12.360
and those people can have
multiple airway infections,

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:14.610
they can have problems with fertility,

00:54:14.610 --> 00:54:18.930
all because of the motors that
move these cilia don't work.

00:54:18.930 --> 00:54:21.330
And in fact, polycystic kidney disease,

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:24.300
the disease that Ty mentioned
that our lab studies

00:54:24.300 --> 00:54:28.173
is also related to
dysfunctions of the cilia.

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:33.850
Finally, coming back to the
kidney, I want you to notice

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:40.130
that this kidney cell has a
huge mitochondria filling,

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:41.790
almost its whole cytoplasm.

00:54:41.790 --> 00:54:44.850
Again, remember these guys need to use

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:48.270
lots and lots of energy
to pump salt and water.

00:54:48.270 --> 00:54:52.590
And you can see that the
membrane of the cell is folded

00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:54.000
around these mitochondria.

00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:59.000
The membrane of these kidney
cells are ruffled, or pleated,

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:02.730
just like if you open a curtain.

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:06.450
If you open a drape, you
get pleats in that drape,

00:55:06.450 --> 00:55:10.470
that increases the surface
area of this cell membrane.

00:55:10.470 --> 00:55:13.500
So this cell has all of these folds

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:15.420
in order to increase its surface area

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:18.690
so that it can carry
more pumping apparatus

00:55:18.690 --> 00:55:21.060
so that it can move that salt and water.

00:55:21.060 --> 00:55:23.910
And look, there are mitochondria sitting

00:55:23.910 --> 00:55:25.710
right next to those pumps,

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:28.260
prepared to deliver energy to the pumps.

00:55:28.260 --> 00:55:32.670
We still don't know how the
mitochondria know to go there.

00:55:32.670 --> 00:55:35.760
One of the things I'm excited
about working on in my lab

00:55:35.760 --> 00:55:39.540
is trying to understand
who tells the mitochondria

00:55:39.540 --> 00:55:41.310
to go to these pleats.

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:42.780
How does the cell know

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:45.663
where it needs its various components?

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:49.290
But another thing that's really exciting

00:55:49.290 --> 00:55:51.090
about the work we do in the lab

00:55:51.090 --> 00:55:54.030
is just how aesthetically beautiful it is.

00:55:54.030 --> 00:55:56.970
These are images of kidney tubules.

00:55:56.970 --> 00:55:59.970
In green, we've stained
for the mitochondria,

00:55:59.970 --> 00:56:03.240
and you can see the striated structures

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:05.100
I told you about before,

00:56:05.100 --> 00:56:08.220
and these cross sections of
kidney tubules correspond

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:10.200
to where the mitochondria are.

00:56:10.200 --> 00:56:12.120
Here's a different view of the same thing.

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:15.660
These green circles,
cross-sections of mitochondria.

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:18.810
Here cells grown in a
three-dimensional matrix

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:20.220
that could form a cyst.

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:23.250
And again, you can see
mitochondria traveling

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:25.353
through the whole length of the cytoplasm.

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:30.390
So for me, what I carry away from this

00:56:30.390 --> 00:56:33.450
is this beautiful idea
that the form of the cell

00:56:33.450 --> 00:56:35.310
very much follows its function,

00:56:35.310 --> 00:56:38.490
and we're just beginning to
understand how that works.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:40.410
We know that all the information you need

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:43.560
to make a cell is present in the nucleus,

00:56:43.560 --> 00:56:45.840
and we're still just learning how it is

00:56:45.840 --> 00:56:49.050
that that information is
interpreted in each cell type

00:56:49.050 --> 00:56:51.960
to make each of these unique structures.

00:56:51.960 --> 00:56:56.100
And looking at these unique
structures is both a joy

00:56:56.100 --> 00:57:00.270
and a privilege because they're
so exquisitely beautiful.

00:57:00.270 --> 00:57:03.663
And bringing me back to the
theme and title of the lecture,

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:07.380
the cells of our bodies have a remarkable,

00:57:07.380 --> 00:57:11.220
a beautiful array of structures
whose distribution allows us

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.740
to maintain our interior milieu

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:16.800
and allow us to carry our worlds with us.

00:57:16.800 --> 00:57:18.630
So at this point, I
wanna thank you very much

00:57:18.630 --> 00:57:19.740
for your attention,

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:22.778
and I'd be happy to answer
any questions you might have.

00:57:22.778 --> 00:57:25.861
(audience clapping)

00:57:32.513 --> 00:57:34.263
- Thank you, Michael.

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:37.140
It was wonderful, I knew it would be.

00:57:37.140 --> 00:57:38.090
Thank you, Michael.

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:40.920
Frederick has the microphone,

00:57:40.920 --> 00:57:43.320
so we just ask that you
use that for your questions

00:57:43.320 --> 00:57:48.270
so all the questions will be
picked up on the video feed.

00:57:48.270 --> 00:57:50.640
If I may begin, though,
with the first one,

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:52.680
I've read, and forgive my ignorance,

00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:55.320
that mitochondria are,

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:58.140
it came to us through
endosymbiosis, right?

00:57:58.140 --> 00:58:01.740
So they were bacteria,
evolutionarily speaking.

00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:06.740
So could you talk then a bit
about how maybe that came to be

00:58:06.780 --> 00:58:11.780
and what ways in which
they might have addressed,

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:18.140
they might might've developed
solutions for their processes?

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:20.970
Is that similar to the
way epithelial cells have

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:24.480
or, because they were bacteria,

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:26.610
is it a totally different kind of solution

00:58:26.610 --> 00:58:27.630
that they developed?

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:29.340
- That's a, sorry.

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:31.089
- So that's a great question,

00:58:31.089 --> 00:58:33.780
and there are a number of
layers of answers to that.

00:58:33.780 --> 00:58:36.720
So what Ty is referring to is that,

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:40.020
if you look carefully
at those mitochondria,

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:42.200
and if I were to show you
an electron micrograph

00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:45.450
of a bacteria, they would
look sort of similar.

00:58:45.450 --> 00:58:49.350
And in fact, at a molecular
level, they are kind of similar.

00:58:49.350 --> 00:58:53.280
In fact, each mitochondria
has its own DNA.

00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:57.330
Each mitochondria has its own
genome that it carries with it

00:58:57.330 --> 00:59:00.990
that encodes some but not all
of the proteins that it needs,

00:59:00.990 --> 00:59:02.790
and that led to the hypothesis

00:59:02.790 --> 00:59:05.670
which has since been abundantly proven

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:10.350
that mitochondria are
bacteria that somehow ended up

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:14.490
being absorbed into and
maintained by our cells.

00:59:14.490 --> 00:59:18.540
So we are, what are called, eukaryotes,

00:59:18.540 --> 00:59:20.663
or our cells are eukaryotic.

00:59:20.663 --> 00:59:23.820
Eukaryotic means cells with nuclei.

00:59:23.820 --> 00:59:27.150
Cells without nuclei are prokaryotes.

00:59:27.150 --> 00:59:31.230
Somewhere early in the
evolution of eukaryotes,

00:59:31.230 --> 00:59:34.620
there were some eukaryotic
cells sitting around,

00:59:34.620 --> 00:59:37.830
a whole bunch of prokaryotic cells,

00:59:37.830 --> 00:59:41.340
and they somehow endocytosed or absorbed

00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:44.790
those prokaryotic cells,
and it occurred to them

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:46.890
that, hey, this works pretty well

00:59:46.890 --> 00:59:49.050
because the prokaryotic cells were able

00:59:49.050 --> 00:59:52.500
to harvest chemical energy
and produce it in ways

00:59:52.500 --> 00:59:53.970
that the cells could use,

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:56.670
and the cells could
provide nutrients and needs

00:59:56.670 --> 00:59:58.620
that the prokaryotes use.

00:59:58.620 --> 01:00:01.590
Many of you may know that in coral reefs,

01:00:01.590 --> 01:00:03.720
the coral polyps have algae

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:06.240
living symbiotically within them.

01:00:06.240 --> 01:00:08.370
It's exactly the same thing.

01:00:08.370 --> 01:00:12.090
The mitochondria that live within us were,

01:00:12.090 --> 01:00:16.020
at one time, free living
bacteria that have now evolved

01:00:16.020 --> 01:00:17.700
so close to being a part of us

01:00:17.700 --> 01:00:19.350
that they couldn't live without us

01:00:19.350 --> 01:00:21.870
and we couldn't live without them.

01:00:21.870 --> 01:00:24.450
Now those mitochondria need to address

01:00:24.450 --> 01:00:27.360
somewhat different problems
than our whole cell.

01:00:27.360 --> 01:00:30.960
They're not concerned with
maintaining the inside of them

01:00:30.960 --> 01:00:34.380
and the outside of them because
they live inside a pouch

01:00:34.380 --> 01:00:38.340
inside a cell, but they
are actually confronted

01:00:38.340 --> 01:00:41.280
with a problem that directly relates

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:43.260
to what they're supposed to do.

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:46.590
They use oxygen to help generate energy.

01:00:46.590 --> 01:00:50.790
It turns out that oxygen is really toxic.

01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:52.440
That doesn't make a lot of sense, right?

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:53.850
We need oxygen to breathe.

01:00:53.850 --> 01:00:54.930
When we're deprived of it,

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:58.020
we start to get really
anxious really fast.

01:00:58.020 --> 01:01:02.190
But it turns out that oxygen
is very chemically reactive,

01:01:02.190 --> 01:01:03.840
and one of the things mitochondria,

01:01:03.840 --> 01:01:06.150
one of the problems they need to solve

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:09.990
is how to prevent themselves
from being damaged

01:01:09.990 --> 01:01:12.150
by the oxygen that they're using.

01:01:12.150 --> 01:01:14.550
And I won't go into
the biochemistry of it,

01:01:14.550 --> 01:01:16.920
but a lot of what mitochondria are doing

01:01:16.920 --> 01:01:21.920
is both perfecting the
mechanisms of energy production,

01:01:23.190 --> 01:01:26.220
and perfecting the mechanisms
of keeping themselves

01:01:26.220 --> 01:01:30.420
from being damaged by
the oxygen that they use.

01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:33.060
These little folds inside the mitochondria

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:36.840
are called cristae, and
within those cristae

01:01:36.840 --> 01:01:41.040
are the molecular machines
that we use to generate energy.

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:44.040
I won't go into the mechanism now,

01:01:44.040 --> 01:01:48.900
but each of those protein complexes

01:01:48.900 --> 01:01:51.960
functions exactly like a water wheel.

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:54.690
The principle by which
our body generates energy

01:01:54.690 --> 01:01:58.500
is exactly the same principle
that's used by water wheels.

01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:00.570
If you drive up to Vermont
and are lucky enough

01:02:00.570 --> 01:02:05.103
to still see an old or recreated
water wheel on an old dam,

01:02:05.940 --> 01:02:08.070
there's a structure that spins,

01:02:08.070 --> 01:02:10.830
and that spinning is exploited

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:13.500
in the same way a
hydroelectric dam exploits

01:02:13.500 --> 01:02:17.130
a spinning turbine to
generate, what's called, ATP,

01:02:17.130 --> 01:02:21.960
which is the chemical form
of energy that our cells use.

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:25.200
So at any level of resolution
that you look at it,

01:02:25.200 --> 01:02:29.160
there are extraordinary
structures, extraordinary machines,

01:02:29.160 --> 01:02:34.160
and the engine that generates
that ATP is very, very similar

01:02:34.980 --> 01:02:36.903
to the one that's used in bacteria.

01:02:47.700 --> 01:02:50.520
- [Audience Member] Hi, what
you were just speaking about,

01:02:50.520 --> 01:02:52.410
I'm not a scientist so I'm gonna say

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:53.910
what you were just speaking about,

01:02:53.910 --> 01:02:56.970
and also there was a
slide where you discussed

01:02:56.970 --> 01:02:59.550
how some people have fertility problems,

01:02:59.550 --> 01:03:04.550
and where the, a little
bit like this center photo,

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:07.350
it was, yeah, no.

01:03:07.350 --> 01:03:08.183
- Oops, sorry.

01:03:08.183 --> 01:03:10.140
- [Audience Member] The
circular one where it's-

01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:10.973
- This guy.

01:03:10.973 --> 01:03:13.290
- Yeah, it's very orderly as far as

01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:17.100
which way these things are
going, it's not random,

01:03:17.100 --> 01:03:19.860
and it kind of reminds me of a lecture

01:03:19.860 --> 01:03:23.700
that was about why is our
heart on the left side,

01:03:23.700 --> 01:03:28.200
and like, you know, everything
else is symmetrical,

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:31.110
and I don't remember the reason why.

01:03:31.110 --> 01:03:34.140
Might've had something to do with gravity,

01:03:34.140 --> 01:03:36.840
and when the embryo develops or something,

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:38.953
so I wondered if you can
speak a little to that.

01:03:38.953 --> 01:03:41.250
- So that is a beautiful question,

01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:45.810
and actually uncovers a very subtle point.

01:03:45.810 --> 01:03:48.930
So you are absolutely right

01:03:48.930 --> 01:03:51.450
that we are mostly bilaterally symmetric.

01:03:51.450 --> 01:03:53.460
Our left hand sort of
looks like a mirror image

01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:56.490
of our right hand, but
there are organs in our body

01:03:56.490 --> 01:04:00.840
that are distributed non uniformly.

01:04:00.840 --> 01:04:02.580
Our heart is on the left side,

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:07.170
our liver is on the right side,
spleen is on the left side.

01:04:07.170 --> 01:04:08.613
How does that happen?

01:04:09.480 --> 01:04:12.033
How do we break that bilateral symmetry?

01:04:13.260 --> 01:04:17.343
It turns out that in the
earliest cells of the embryo,

01:04:18.210 --> 01:04:22.170
these cilia beat, and
they beat in a structure

01:04:22.170 --> 01:04:25.890
called Kupffer's vesicles
to move fluid around

01:04:25.890 --> 01:04:28.230
in a precise direction.

01:04:28.230 --> 01:04:30.690
It turns out that the
movement of that fluid

01:04:30.690 --> 01:04:34.110
in a precise direction,
only in one direction,

01:04:34.110 --> 01:04:38.643
is absolutely required to
create our body asymmetry.

01:04:40.110 --> 01:04:42.660
And actually beautiful work
is done on that at Yale

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:45.630
by Mustafa Khokha and by Martina Bruckner,

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:47.550
who are understanding the mechanisms

01:04:47.550 --> 01:04:51.150
by which the beating
that's created by the cilia

01:04:51.150 --> 01:04:55.110
creates a movement of fluid
in this tiny little fishbowl

01:04:55.110 --> 01:04:59.220
at the tip of the embryo
that's sensed by antennae,

01:04:59.220 --> 01:05:01.500
literally that stick up into it,

01:05:01.500 --> 01:05:03.450
and the bending of those antennae

01:05:03.450 --> 01:05:05.740
in response to the movement of the fluid

01:05:09.030 --> 01:05:13.560
by these cilia is required
for our asymmetry.

01:05:13.560 --> 01:05:16.440
And people who have genetic problems

01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:20.460
that can't make their cilia move

01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:24.990
end up with body parts on the wrong side.

01:05:24.990 --> 01:05:27.750
So you can end up actually
with your heart on the right

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:29.490
and your liver on the left.

01:05:29.490 --> 01:05:33.390
As long as it happens completely,

01:05:33.390 --> 01:05:35.790
you can live a perfectly normal life

01:05:35.790 --> 01:05:38.167
and surprise your friends
at parties by saying,

01:05:38.167 --> 01:05:42.060
"Hey, listen to my heart,"
and they don't hear it

01:05:42.060 --> 01:05:43.623
'cause it's on the other side.

01:05:44.550 --> 01:05:49.550
So these ciliary dyskinesias
can produce complete breakdowns

01:05:49.650 --> 01:05:53.073
or reversals of bilateral symmetry.

01:05:54.570 --> 01:05:57.180
So the cilia are critically important

01:05:57.180 --> 01:06:00.510
to not only moving
things through the airway

01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:04.230
and the oviduct, but also
during embryonic development.

01:06:04.230 --> 01:06:05.780
So that was a very good memory.

01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:09.711
- [Audience Member]
Actually, just to follow up

01:06:09.711 --> 01:06:11.236
on that (speaks faintly),

01:06:11.236 --> 01:06:13.357
but what it is sort of (speaks faintly)

01:06:13.357 --> 01:06:18.357
about the inside and the
outside of existence,

01:06:18.360 --> 01:06:20.880
sort of, because in that lecture,

01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:25.880
they were trying to figure
out why, what's inside of us,

01:06:26.940 --> 01:06:28.890
it must have some relation, I mean,

01:06:28.890 --> 01:06:32.460
that's like the center of
gravity, like the gravity pull.

01:06:32.460 --> 01:06:35.530
Like, it has some relation
to something that's outside

01:06:36.690 --> 01:06:39.390
why the heart is where it
is, if that makes sense.

01:06:39.390 --> 01:06:44.010
Like, there's some interaction
between we are beings

01:06:44.010 --> 01:06:46.680
on an earth with a gravity pull,

01:06:46.680 --> 01:06:49.683
so is there anything like that?

01:06:50.610 --> 01:06:51.720
- Yeah, kind of.

01:06:51.720 --> 01:06:56.720
So when we're standing up,

01:06:58.020 --> 01:07:01.410
it's hard for the blood
that's pumped by our heart

01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:05.220
down to our legs to come
back up to the heart.

01:07:05.220 --> 01:07:10.220
So in fact, when we walk around,

01:07:10.260 --> 01:07:13.230
the movement of our muscles,
that squishing movement

01:07:13.230 --> 01:07:17.190
helps squeeze blood out of the
veins back towards the heart.

01:07:17.190 --> 01:07:19.560
If you sit for a long
time, like in a lecture

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:22.113
where someone is talking
for too long, like I am,

01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:24.630
blood can cool in your feet,

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:26.550
and I don't know if
you've ever read the back

01:07:26.550 --> 01:07:29.220
of the airplane magazines
where it tells you

01:07:29.220 --> 01:07:32.310
on a long flight you're supposed
to move your legs around

01:07:32.310 --> 01:07:36.390
so that the blood doesn't pool
in your legs and form a clot.

01:07:36.390 --> 01:07:40.980
It is absolutely true
that gravity is a force

01:07:40.980 --> 01:07:42.630
that we need to contend with

01:07:42.630 --> 01:07:45.420
in order to maintain our circulation.

01:07:45.420 --> 01:07:48.510
When you lie down, it's
much easier for fluid

01:07:48.510 --> 01:07:52.230
to come back to the heart
than when you're standing up.

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:54.360
Some people who have
trouble with their hearts,

01:07:54.360 --> 01:07:56.850
who have something called heart failure,

01:07:56.850 --> 01:07:59.880
will find that fluid can
build up in their legs

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:02.670
during the day, and when they lie down,

01:08:02.670 --> 01:08:07.020
that fluid moves out of the
legs and returns to the heart,

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:08.790
and actually gets sent to the kidneys,

01:08:08.790 --> 01:08:10.290
and they find that they have to get up

01:08:10.290 --> 01:08:12.750
in the middle of the night
to urinate many times a night

01:08:12.750 --> 01:08:14.940
because the fluid that was in their legs

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:17.190
is now being delivered back to the heart,

01:08:17.190 --> 01:08:19.267
is being delivered to
the kidneys, which say,

01:08:19.267 --> 01:08:20.730
"Hey, there's too much fluid here.

01:08:20.730 --> 01:08:22.380
We need to get rid of some."

01:08:22.380 --> 01:08:27.380
So gravity plays a big
part in the specializations

01:08:27.540 --> 01:08:30.700
that our body has developed
to move things around

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:34.710
in a world that isn't
completely symmetrical,

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:36.480
if that makes sense.

01:08:36.480 --> 01:08:37.313
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:08:37.313 --> 01:08:38.146
- Sure.

01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:45.090
(audience member speaking faintly)

01:08:45.090 --> 01:08:45.923
Hi, good to see you.

01:08:45.923 --> 01:08:47.010
- [Ty] Do you need the microphone?

01:08:47.010 --> 01:08:47.843
- [Audience Member] Yes.

01:08:48.690 --> 01:08:52.470
Hi Mike, have you talked
about nephrotic syndrome?

01:08:52.470 --> 01:08:53.303
- So I didn't.

01:08:53.303 --> 01:08:56.970
So that's a very interesting topic

01:08:56.970 --> 01:08:58.953
that I didn't get time to talk about.

01:09:00.853 --> 01:09:04.140
Remember, I said that we have
a million of these glomeruli

01:09:04.140 --> 01:09:05.433
in each of our kidneys.

01:09:06.450 --> 01:09:08.880
Those glomeruli filter the blood

01:09:08.880 --> 01:09:11.220
and produce 180 liters of filtrate.

01:09:11.220 --> 01:09:15.300
Now, your blood has salt water
in it, it has cells in it,

01:09:15.300 --> 01:09:17.050
and it has a lot of proteins in it.

01:09:18.420 --> 01:09:21.780
The kidney filtrate has
the salt and the water,

01:09:21.780 --> 01:09:24.120
but none of the blood cells
and none of the proteins.

01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:27.150
So you've got an amazing
filter in your kidney

01:09:27.150 --> 01:09:29.940
that can filter out all of the protein

01:09:29.940 --> 01:09:31.533
and let the salt water through.

01:09:32.640 --> 01:09:34.960
There's a condition
called nephrotic syndrome

01:09:35.820 --> 01:09:38.730
where the ability of that glomerulus

01:09:38.730 --> 01:09:41.400
to filter out the protein breaks down,

01:09:41.400 --> 01:09:46.110
and now the protein can
end up in the urine.

01:09:46.110 --> 01:09:48.810
Now, that wouldn't be such
a big problem by itself,

01:09:48.810 --> 01:09:51.900
except we need that protein in our blood

01:09:51.900 --> 01:09:55.383
because that protein helps
keep fluid in our capillaries.

01:09:56.250 --> 01:09:58.320
There's a complicated mechanism

01:09:58.320 --> 01:10:01.590
whereby the presence of
protein in the capillaries

01:10:01.590 --> 01:10:05.280
is critically important to
determining the balance of fluid

01:10:05.280 --> 01:10:08.700
in our capillaries versus in our tissues.

01:10:08.700 --> 01:10:11.370
If we lose the protein in our capillaries,

01:10:11.370 --> 01:10:13.860
fluid can flow out into our tissues

01:10:13.860 --> 01:10:16.803
and produce huge swelling,
what's called edema.

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:21.990
Patients with nephrotic
syndrome, usually it's children,

01:10:21.990 --> 01:10:25.260
will wake up one morning
and be completely swollen.

01:10:25.260 --> 01:10:27.870
They look like the
Stay-Puft Marshmallow Man

01:10:27.870 --> 01:10:31.110
because now they can't keep
fluid in their capillaries.

01:10:31.110 --> 01:10:33.930
It flows out into their tissues.

01:10:33.930 --> 01:10:36.030
Fortunately, it's usually self-limited,

01:10:36.030 --> 01:10:37.650
can be treated with steroids,

01:10:37.650 --> 01:10:40.623
and it goes away as
mysteriously as it comes,

01:10:41.520 --> 01:10:44.010
but most of us have probably seen

01:10:44.010 --> 01:10:48.240
the very horrifying pictures
of children who are starving,

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:51.060
a condition called kwashiorkor.

01:10:51.060 --> 01:10:52.860
Have you ever wondered why those children

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:55.140
have enormously big bellies?

01:10:55.140 --> 01:10:56.190
These kids are starving.

01:10:56.190 --> 01:10:58.230
Why are their bellies so big?

01:10:58.230 --> 01:11:01.770
It turns out that they
don't eat enough amino acids

01:11:01.770 --> 01:11:03.060
to make proteins.

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:05.460
They don't have enough
proteins in their blood

01:11:05.460 --> 01:11:07.710
to keep the fluid in their capillaries,

01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:10.680
and the fluid ends up
pooling in their abdomen.

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:13.170
So it's the same mechanism
as nephrotic syndrome,

01:11:13.170 --> 01:11:17.130
except in nephrotic syndrome,
protein is being urinated out,

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:21.510
in protein calorie malnutrition,
protein isn't being made.

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:24.510
But there's a critical physiologic force

01:11:24.510 --> 01:11:29.010
called the Starling force
whereby protein is necessary

01:11:29.010 --> 01:11:30.840
to keep fluid in capillaries,

01:11:30.840 --> 01:11:34.890
and that's lost in nephrotic
syndrome when protein is lost

01:11:34.890 --> 01:11:35.723
in the urine.

01:11:37.080 --> 01:11:38.580
So thank you for the question.

01:11:40.830 --> 01:11:42.480
Mother.

01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:45.480
(audience laughing)

01:11:49.740 --> 01:11:51.377
Yes, is the answer.

01:11:51.377 --> 01:11:54.460
(audience laughing)

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:58.140
- [Mother] First of all,
I'm very proud of you.

01:11:58.140 --> 01:11:58.987
- Thanks you.

01:11:59.908 --> 01:12:01.666
(audience clapping)

01:12:01.666 --> 01:12:04.740
- [Mother] Secondly, I have always been,

01:12:04.740 --> 01:12:09.227
since I was a child, very
fascinated by Darwin,

01:12:10.320 --> 01:12:15.320
and how we evolved
because we needed things.

01:12:16.260 --> 01:12:20.400
How can you take a
structure like the kidney

01:12:20.400 --> 01:12:24.240
or even what you
described that's so minute

01:12:24.240 --> 01:12:29.013
and apparently so important,
and how does the body know,

01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:34.880
or how did the evolutionary workings know

01:12:35.040 --> 01:12:39.930
that this little minute thing
that goes around in a circle

01:12:39.930 --> 01:12:43.219
is necessary to do the next thing?

01:12:43.219 --> 01:12:44.052
- Right.

01:12:44.052 --> 01:12:46.320
So the question is how
could something so beautiful

01:12:46.320 --> 01:12:51.090
and intricate arise from
random experimentation,

01:12:51.090 --> 01:12:54.690
and right, this is the question
that motivates the debate

01:12:54.690 --> 01:12:59.400
between intelligent design
and Darwinian evolution.

01:12:59.400 --> 01:13:02.430
And the answer is, and there's
very good evidence for it,

01:13:02.430 --> 01:13:05.310
that this happens by a lot, a lot, a lot

01:13:05.310 --> 01:13:09.810
of random experimentation,
random mutation over time

01:13:09.810 --> 01:13:13.290
that produces huge numbers
of different changes,

01:13:13.290 --> 01:13:17.490
most of which don't work,
but the ones that do work

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:21.660
give each cell or each
organism a selective advantage

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:25.290
that allows it to use that
structure to do better,

01:13:25.290 --> 01:13:27.630
and that structure can then get improved

01:13:27.630 --> 01:13:30.300
by the next change or
mutation, et cetera, et cetera.

01:13:30.300 --> 01:13:33.570
So in response to Ty's question,

01:13:33.570 --> 01:13:38.570
I mentioned that these
mitochondria used to be bacteria.

01:13:41.460 --> 01:13:45.990
Bacteria have a lifespan
that's measured in,

01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:47.730
you know, hours to days, right?

01:13:47.730 --> 01:13:49.620
So bacteria should not invest

01:13:49.620 --> 01:13:52.500
in long-term savings bonds or anything.

01:13:52.500 --> 01:13:55.200
They're turning over
really, really quickly.

01:13:55.200 --> 01:13:58.290
They're dividing incredibly quickly.

01:13:58.290 --> 01:14:01.590
They undergo huge numbers of mutations,

01:14:01.590 --> 01:14:04.110
random changes in their DNA.

01:14:04.110 --> 01:14:07.860
Most of those mutations don't
do these bacteria any good,

01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:12.420
and their bacteria offspring
are not going to succeed,

01:14:12.420 --> 01:14:15.450
but the ones that do succeed,
that build a better way

01:14:15.450 --> 01:14:18.720
to harvest energy, to make chemical energy

01:14:18.720 --> 01:14:20.670
that cells can use, they're gonna have

01:14:20.670 --> 01:14:25.290
a huge selective advantage
and they're going to become

01:14:25.290 --> 01:14:28.350
the most important bacteria in the slime.

01:14:28.350 --> 01:14:33.350
And so those mechanisms
have been proven beautifully

01:14:33.960 --> 01:14:35.880
through, what we call, directed evolution.

01:14:35.880 --> 01:14:38.610
There's a whole science
now that was started

01:14:38.610 --> 01:14:40.320
by a woman named Frances Arnold,

01:14:40.320 --> 01:14:43.117
who won the Nobel Prize
for this, who said,

01:14:43.117 --> 01:14:45.360
"Well, if evolution can work this way,

01:14:45.360 --> 01:14:48.060
maybe we can use it for our advantage."

01:14:48.060 --> 01:14:52.080
Maybe we can convince
bacteria to do new things

01:14:52.080 --> 01:14:54.870
that they couldn't do
before, like eat oil spills,

01:14:54.870 --> 01:14:59.580
or chew up plastic, or
make proteins that we need

01:14:59.580 --> 01:15:01.473
for biomedical uses.

01:15:03.000 --> 01:15:05.220
So what she and her
colleagues and the people

01:15:05.220 --> 01:15:07.830
who have followed
through on the principles

01:15:07.830 --> 01:15:12.830
that she developed have done
is to just expose bacteria

01:15:14.340 --> 01:15:16.473
to chemicals that would mutagenize them,

01:15:17.340 --> 01:15:20.670
and then come up with a way
of selecting for the bacteria

01:15:20.670 --> 01:15:22.770
that have the properties they want,

01:15:22.770 --> 01:15:23.910
and saying, "Look, I don't care

01:15:23.910 --> 01:15:26.190
how you figure this out, bacteria,

01:15:26.190 --> 01:15:30.360
but I want one of you to
figure out how to chew up oil.

01:15:30.360 --> 01:15:32.160
Get back to me when you've done it."

01:15:33.360 --> 01:15:36.570
And so we can actually speed up evolution

01:15:36.570 --> 01:15:39.630
by increasing the rate
of random mutagenesis,

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:42.450
and increasing the rate
of natural selection.

01:15:42.450 --> 01:15:45.060
So not only does it work,

01:15:45.060 --> 01:15:47.340
not only is that the way this happened,

01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:50.673
but we can actually engineer
it and use it to our advantage.

01:15:51.810 --> 01:15:52.883
- [Audience Member] A little scary.

01:15:55.320 --> 01:15:57.946
- Yeah, everything
nowadays is a little scary.

01:15:57.946 --> 01:16:01.029
(audience laughing)

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:10.262
- [Ty] Would you go
back to the cilia slide?

01:16:10.262 --> 01:16:11.095
- Sure.

01:16:11.095 --> 01:16:13.200
- [Ty] Everyone's favorite
slide, apparently.

01:16:15.120 --> 01:16:18.000
Are cilia throughout the body

01:16:18.000 --> 01:16:20.460
thusly organized with the ring of nine?

01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:21.600
Are all cilia like this?

01:16:21.600 --> 01:16:25.020
And they're distinct
from microtubules, right?

01:16:25.020 --> 01:16:28.530
Do microtubules in
cross-section look the same way,

01:16:28.530 --> 01:16:31.680
or do they differ depending
on their location?

01:16:31.680 --> 01:16:36.680
- Right, so here are
microtubules in cross-section,

01:16:38.220 --> 01:16:42.390
and you can see that there are
parallel arrays of filaments

01:16:42.390 --> 01:16:45.660
that happen to be made up
of a protein called actin

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:47.370
that fills these filaments,

01:16:47.370 --> 01:16:51.393
about a few dozen of them per microtubule.

01:16:52.500 --> 01:16:55.590
Microvilli can move,
but nowhere near as well

01:16:55.590 --> 01:16:58.053
and nowhere near as coordinated as cilia.

01:16:59.970 --> 01:17:03.660
Cilia are made up of these
arrays of microtubules

01:17:03.660 --> 01:17:06.330
that are made up of a
protein called tubulin,

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:08.640
and connected by radial spokes

01:17:08.640 --> 01:17:10.623
that hold those tubules together,

01:17:11.460 --> 01:17:15.600
and motors called dynein
and kinesin that use energy

01:17:15.600 --> 01:17:20.600
to make these motor, these
tubules beat in a systematic way.

01:17:22.230 --> 01:17:24.390
There are two kinds of cilia in your body.

01:17:24.390 --> 01:17:27.630
There are the movable cilia
that we call motile cilia,

01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:31.440
that have nine rings of
tubules and a central pair.

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:33.780
And then there are non-motile cilia,

01:17:33.780 --> 01:17:37.893
what we call the,

01:17:39.180 --> 01:17:40.680
well, the non motile cilia,

01:17:40.680 --> 01:17:43.680
which is present in almost
every cell in our body.

01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:47.190
It doesn't beat, but it
acts like an antenna.

01:17:47.190 --> 01:17:50.550
In fact, in the kidney,
each cell has a cilia

01:17:50.550 --> 01:17:52.680
that's sticking out into the lumen,

01:17:52.680 --> 01:17:54.390
and as fluid flows past it,

01:17:54.390 --> 01:17:56.343
it bends like a reed in a stream,

01:17:57.240 --> 01:18:02.240
and screwing up the
sensing of that fluid flow

01:18:02.280 --> 01:18:05.640
is part of what leads to
polycystic kidney disease.

01:18:05.640 --> 01:18:07.350
And beautiful work on that has been done

01:18:07.350 --> 01:18:10.290
by Steve Somlo here at Yale, among others.

01:18:10.290 --> 01:18:14.730
But sensory cilia or
primary cilia are involved

01:18:14.730 --> 01:18:18.150
in sensing chemicals,
sensing physical stimuli.

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:22.290
So the cells in our eye that detect light,

01:18:22.290 --> 01:18:25.620
the light sensors are
actually a specialized cilium.

01:18:25.620 --> 01:18:27.930
In our nose, the chemo sensors

01:18:27.930 --> 01:18:31.590
that detect smells are
a specialized cilia.

01:18:31.590 --> 01:18:34.890
So there are mechanically and
chemically sensitive cilia

01:18:34.890 --> 01:18:36.120
that look just like this,

01:18:36.120 --> 01:18:39.660
except they don't have the
central pair and don't move.

01:18:39.660 --> 01:18:43.410
But a subset of tissues in
our body have motile cilia,

01:18:43.410 --> 01:18:46.140
like the airway, like the oviduct,

01:18:46.140 --> 01:18:49.350
and those use cilia in a different way

01:18:49.350 --> 01:18:52.563
to create this fluid flow,
to create this motion.

01:18:59.324 --> 01:19:02.460
(audience member speaking faintly)

01:19:02.460 --> 01:19:04.360
- [Audience Member] And it's a little,

01:19:05.280 --> 01:19:09.180
like a little misty, foggy, unconnected,

01:19:09.180 --> 01:19:11.670
but you had a slide,

01:19:11.670 --> 01:19:14.220
well, it's basically the question about

01:19:14.220 --> 01:19:19.020
why is kidney disease, nephrititis or,

01:19:19.020 --> 01:19:21.750
isn't that the root is death, where,

01:19:21.750 --> 01:19:25.830
you know, other diseases, you
have appendicitis or pancrea,

01:19:25.830 --> 01:19:30.600
but you had a cell facet, it was purple,

01:19:30.600 --> 01:19:32.430
that was had the neph.

01:19:32.430 --> 01:19:37.140
I just wondered, is there
relation in the naming of things

01:19:37.140 --> 01:19:40.350
that kidney disease was actually equated

01:19:40.350 --> 01:19:42.600
with like sure death
or something, whereas-

01:19:42.600 --> 01:19:47.600
- Yeah, well, so prior to 1960-something,

01:19:48.480 --> 01:19:52.440
if you lost kidney function,
you were gonna die.

01:19:52.440 --> 01:19:55.020
There was not a lot that
could be done for you.

01:19:55.020 --> 01:19:58.140
In the 1960s, renal dialysis was developed

01:19:58.140 --> 01:20:00.750
so that people who had kidney failure,

01:20:00.750 --> 01:20:03.720
the functions of the kidney
to get rid of waste products

01:20:03.720 --> 01:20:07.860
and to maintain salt and water
balance could be maintained

01:20:07.860 --> 01:20:11.850
by dialyzing the blood, that
is running it through a machine

01:20:11.850 --> 01:20:13.830
and doing the same cleaning process

01:20:13.830 --> 01:20:15.840
that the kidney normally does.

01:20:15.840 --> 01:20:17.880
And then subsequent to that,

01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:20.460
the development of
transplantation medicine

01:20:20.460 --> 01:20:24.990
allowed the replacement
of an ailing kidney

01:20:24.990 --> 01:20:25.950
with a donor kidney,

01:20:25.950 --> 01:20:28.770
if someone is fortunate enough to get one,

01:20:28.770 --> 01:20:33.150
but the kidney is far and
away not the only organ

01:20:33.150 --> 01:20:37.140
whose dysfunction can lead
to serious disease or death.

01:20:37.140 --> 01:20:39.450
I mean, obviously,
that's true of the heart,

01:20:39.450 --> 01:20:41.040
that's true of the brain.

01:20:41.040 --> 01:20:43.260
It's also true even of the pancreas.

01:20:43.260 --> 01:20:46.830
If you lose the ability
to secrete insulin,

01:20:46.830 --> 01:20:49.680
that could limit your
lifespan basically to weeks,

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:52.950
and back in the days before
the discovery of insulin,

01:20:52.950 --> 01:20:57.950
there was no way to treat someone
who had type one diabetes.

01:20:58.890 --> 01:21:03.780
In fact, the first discussion
of the discovery of insulin

01:21:03.780 --> 01:21:08.250
and its ability to reverse the
symptoms of type one diabetes

01:21:08.250 --> 01:21:11.790
was a lecture delivered
here at Yale 102 years ago

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:15.540
by Banting of Banting and Best,
the discoverers from Toronto

01:21:15.540 --> 01:21:18.390
who discovered insulin
and presented it at Yale

01:21:18.390 --> 01:21:21.390
at a meeting of the American
Physiological Society.

01:21:21.390 --> 01:21:24.090
So Yale played a minor role

01:21:24.090 --> 01:21:26.250
in at least disseminating that discovery,

01:21:26.250 --> 01:21:29.190
if not in the making of the discovery.

01:21:29.190 --> 01:21:32.220
So yeah, I mean, we
have lots of body organs

01:21:32.220 --> 01:21:33.870
and we need them.

01:21:33.870 --> 01:21:35.520
They each do different things.

01:21:35.520 --> 01:21:38.820
Just like the different
components of a cell are required

01:21:38.820 --> 01:21:41.010
to keep that cell working and healthy,

01:21:41.010 --> 01:21:42.630
each of our organs are required

01:21:42.630 --> 01:21:44.913
to keep the whole body
working and healthy.

01:21:46.205 --> 01:21:47.105
- [Audience Member] So is there something

01:21:47.105 --> 01:21:50.278
about why the purple
one we call the nephros?

01:21:50.278 --> 01:21:52.650
Is that a coincidence, or is it-

01:21:52.650 --> 01:21:56.400
- So the Greek word for kidney is nephros,

01:21:56.400 --> 01:21:58.290
and the structures,

01:21:58.290 --> 01:22:01.173
the tubules of the kidney
are called nephrons,

01:22:02.040 --> 01:22:05.280
so you have a million nephrons
in each of your kidney.

01:22:05.280 --> 01:22:09.360
Those nephrons are long
tubes of epithelial cells

01:22:09.360 --> 01:22:12.183
that carry out all of this
salt and water transport.

01:22:14.280 --> 01:22:15.113
Sure.

01:22:16.190 --> 01:22:17.700
- [Ty] And I think the root

01:22:17.700 --> 01:22:21.060
maybe you were thinking of
is necro for death, right?

01:22:21.060 --> 01:22:23.190
Like necrosis, like necrotic.

01:22:23.190 --> 01:22:24.023
- [Audience Member] Yeah.

01:22:24.023 --> 01:22:26.220
- [Ty] So that's a Greek root for death.

01:22:26.220 --> 01:22:27.330
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:22:27.330 --> 01:22:29.763
- [Ty] Okay, any other questions?

01:22:31.500 --> 01:22:32.880
Okay, well thank you.

01:22:32.880 --> 01:22:34.230
Please join me again.

01:22:34.230 --> 01:22:37.666
Thank you, Michael, for a wonderful talk.

01:22:37.666 --> 01:22:40.784
(audience clapping)

01:22:40.784 --> 01:22:43.451
(gentle music)

